      "text": "Let's use combinatorial counting to determine the probability that Terry and Mary both end up with the same color combination of candies.\n\nFirst, we need to calculate the total number of ways to pick 2 candies out of 20. The number of ways to choose 2 candies from 20 is given by the combination formula \\(\\binom{20}{2}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{20}{2} = \\frac{20 \\times 19}{2 \\times 1} = 190\n\\]\n\nThis is the total number of ways Terry can pick 2 candies from the 20 candies.\n\nNext, we need to consider the two possible color combinations that Terry could pick: 2 red candies or 2 blue candies. The number of ways to pick 2 red candies from the 10 red candies is \\(\\binom{10}{2}\\), and the number of ways to pick 2 blue candies from the 10 blue candies is \\(\\binom{10}{2}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2 \\times 1} = 45\n\\]\n\nSo, the total number of ways Terry can pick 2 red candies or 2 blue candies is:\n\n\\[\n45 + 45 = 90\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to determine the probability that Mary picks the same color combination as Terry. If Terry picks 2 red candies, there are \\(18\\) red candies left and \\(10\\) blue candies and \\(10\\) red candies together. The number of ways to pick 2 candies from the remaining 18 red candies or 18 blue candies (both scenarios are symmetric) is \\(\\binom{18}{2}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{18}{2} = \\frac{18 \\times 17}{2 \\times 1} = 153\n\\]\n\nIf Terry picks 2 blue candies, there are \\(\\binom{18}{2}\\) ways to pick 2 blue candies from the remaining \\(18\\) blue candies or \\(18\\) red candies (again, both are the same number of ways) which is also:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{18}{2} = 153\n\\]\n\nSo, the total number of favorable outcomes for Mary to pick the same color combination as Terry is:\n\n\\[\n153 + 153 = 306\n\\]\n\nThe probability that Terry and Mary both pick the same color combination is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of ways Terry can pick 2 candies:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{306}{190}\n\\]\n\nWe simplify this fraction by finding the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 306 and 190, which is 2:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{306 \\div 2}{190 \\div 2} = \\frac{153}{95}\n\\]\n\nThe fraction \\(\\frac{153}{95}\\) is in its simplest form because 153 and 95 are relatively prime. Therefore, the probability is \\(\\frac{153}{95}\\), and the sum of the numerator and the denominator is:\n\n\\[\n153 + 95 = 248\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{248}\n\\]",
