      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the probability that Terry and Mary pick the same color combination of candies. Let's break it down step by step.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the total number of ways to pick candies\nFirst, we calculate the total number of ways to pick 2 candies out of 20. This is given by the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!} \\):\n\\[\n\\binom{20}{2} = \\frac{20!}{2!18!} = \\frac{20 \\times 19}{2 \\times 1} = 190\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the number of ways Terry can pick two candies of the same color\n#### Red candies\nThe number of ways Terry can pick 2 red candies out of 10 is:\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10!}{2!8!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2 \\times 1} = 45\n\\]\n\n#### Blue candies\nThe number of ways Terry can pick 2 blue candies out of 10 is:\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10!}{2!8!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2 \\times 1} = 45\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Total number of ways Terry can pick the same color combination\nTerry can pick either 2 red or 2 blue candies, so the total number of ways he can pick the same color combination is:\n\\[\n45 + 45 = 90\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Calculate the number of ways Mary can pick the same color combination\nOnce Terry has picked his candies, there are \\(20 - 2 = 18\\) candies left, with 8 red and 10 blue candies remaining. We need to calculate the number of ways Mary can pick 2 candies of the same color from these 18 candies.\n\n#### Red candies\nThe number of ways Mary can pick 2 red candies out of 8 is:\n\\[\n\\binom{8}{2} = \\frac{8!}{2!6!} = \\frac{8 \\times 7}{2 \\times 1} = 28\n\\]\n\n#### Blue candies\nThe number of ways Mary can pick 2 blue candies out of 10 is:\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10!}{2!8!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2 \\times 1} = 45\n\\]\n\n### Step 5: Total number of ways Mary can pick the same color combination\nMary can pick either 2 red or 2 blue candies, so the total number of ways she can pick the same color combination is:\n\\[\n28 + 45 = 73\n\\]\n\n### Step 6: Calculate the probability\nThe probability that Terry and Mary pick the same color combination is the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes to the total number of outcomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{90}{190} = \\frac{9}{19}\n\\]\n\n### Step 7: Simplify the fraction\nThe fraction \\(\\frac{9}{19}\\) is already in its simplest form, so \\(m = 9\\) and \\(n = 19\\). Therefore, \\(m + n = 9 + 19 = 28\\).\n\n### Final Answer\n\\[\n\\boxed{28}\n\\]",
